1
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,880
So lecture three is
about verification.

2
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:14,560
In the last lecture when we
talked about inference time

3
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:20,320
scaling and the generation
verification gap,

4
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:24,040
basically what we discussed
was that language model seems

5
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:29,000
to be able to seems to
know the answer to many

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,680
of the hard questions, and
especially with methods

7
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:36,120
such as repeated sampling,
or other scaling test time

8
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:38,260
techniques, they
can generate one.

9
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:41,060
But a question is, how do they.

10
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:45,040
How can we automatically
select which answer is correct

11
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,840
or guide the model
throughout the process

12
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:49,580
of answer generation.

13
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,200
So that leads us to
verification where

14
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:55,480
which we are
learning about today.

15
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:58,040
So in today's
lecture, we are going

16
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:02,450
to learn about the
following four papers.

17
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:07,670
And there is a
progression of how the way

18
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:10,830
we approach verification
changed or progressed

19
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:12,790
throughout the years.

20
00:01:12,790 --> 00:01:15,470
So let's first
start with training

21
00:01:15,470 --> 00:01:18,850
verifiers to solve
math problems.

22
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:23,990
This is a paper
from OpenAI in 2021.

23
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:26,950
And the motivation
for this fork was

24
00:01:26,950 --> 00:01:32,990
that LLMs hallucinate and
can confidently present

25
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:35,390
wrong solutions to the users.

26
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:37,210
This still is true to this day.

27
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:39,070
Four years later,
of course, the model

28
00:01:39,070 --> 00:01:42,310
have become
significantly better.

29
00:01:42,310 --> 00:01:45,950
There is also at the time,
one of the contributions

30
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:50,230
of this paper was to introduce
a new reasoning benchmark

31
00:01:50,230 --> 00:01:51,630
around math.

32
00:01:51,630 --> 00:01:55,910
And you can see here in
blue one of these problems.

33
00:01:55,910 --> 00:02:04,780
This is the type of problem that
at the time it was hard for LLMs

34
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:05,820
to solve.

35
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:08,840
And this led to
this new dataset.

36
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:17,380
And that has become
kind of a big part

37
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:20,280
of many of the benchmarking
tasks for LLMs.

38
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:24,040
And even to this day, this
dataset is called GSM 8-K.

39
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,500
And even to this date, for
smaller models and benchmarking,

40
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:32,560
it's still a very useful
language modeling evaluation.

41
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,700
And you can consider it
for the type of evals

42
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:38,660
that you want to define
on your projects as well.

43
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:43,940
So it basically consists
of 8,500 grade school math

44
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:45,380
problems.

45
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:49,780
And the goal was for this
was to have the quality

46
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:51,720
and diversity of the problems.

47
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:56,550
But also importantly there was
a focus on multi-step reasoning,

48
00:02:56,550 --> 00:02:58,470
meaning that to
solve the problems,

49
00:02:58,470 --> 00:03:00,870
even though the
problems were simple.

50
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:04,290
But the model required
a few steps of reasoning

51
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:07,650
to get to the answer.

52
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:13,970
And you could also collect the
solution in natural language as

53
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:18,450
opposed to pure math.

54
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:20,950
But the other contribution
of this paper,

55
00:03:20,950 --> 00:03:23,390
which we are going to
spend more time on,

56
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:26,490
was to train a
verification model

57
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:31,370
where the verifier outputs the
probability that a solution is

58
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:32,530
correct.

59
00:03:32,530 --> 00:03:35,350
So let's see about how
this can be useful.

60
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:40,990
For example, the model that
as humans we solve problems.

61
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:42,930
It would be very,
very good for us

62
00:03:42,930 --> 00:03:45,530
to have access to
a rubric or a way

63
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:48,430
for us to tell whether
we are correct or not.

64
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:50,810
And we kind of want to
provide the same thing

65
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:55,160
through this verifier
for the language model.

66
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:59,920
So now how these the verifier
was trained in this case

67
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,840
is that assume that we have a
question and a solution for it.

68
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:09,760
Then we do have a label whether
is a binary label, whether it's

69
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:11,680
correct or incorrect.

70
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:15,760
And during the training process,
the generator, the language

71
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:19,040
model, which is in charge
of answering questions

72
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:25,400
here produces PSI, which is
the solution to question QI,

73
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:27,240
and then we can generate--

74
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,780
do repeated sampling
and generate multiple,

75
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:32,880
in this case, 100
solutions per problem.

76
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:34,880
And then we create
these labels based

77
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:37,480
on whether they
were correct or not.

78
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:41,920
Remember that these questions
and answers were selectively

79
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:45,340
designed by a lot of
manual human supervision.

80
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:48,080
So we know what the
answers-- the final answers

81
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:49,480
to these problems are.

82
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:52,440
So we can just, based on
that, create these labels

83
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:54,710
for these problems.

84
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:57,750
And then given that we
can train a verifier,

85
00:04:57,750 --> 00:05:01,610
because now, we have a
question, we have a solution,

86
00:05:01,610 --> 00:05:04,330
and then we have a label for it.

87
00:05:07,750 --> 00:05:09,910
Now, the way we
use it at test time

88
00:05:09,910 --> 00:05:13,490
is that at test time, again,
we generate a lot of answers.

89
00:05:13,490 --> 00:05:15,070
And then we use the
verifier and look

90
00:05:15,070 --> 00:05:18,710
at the score, basically,
the log probability that

91
00:05:18,710 --> 00:05:22,550
is in this model now, to see
which one has a higher score

92
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:24,590
and use that for as our--

93
00:05:24,590 --> 00:05:27,170
and show that as
our final answer.

94
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:34,870
So in this paper, the verifier
was trained not just based

95
00:05:34,870 --> 00:05:37,950
on the correctness
prediction, but there was also

96
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:41,010
a language modeling objective.

97
00:05:41,010 --> 00:05:42,630
So the language
modeling objective

98
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:48,230
is also given, for example here,
given these tokens corresponding

99
00:05:48,230 --> 00:05:53,000
to the questions, the moment
we get to the solution token,

100
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,620
we also add a loss that
kind of urges-- like,

101
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:59,420
motivates the model
to predict or reduce

102
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:08,560
the solution distance from the
true labels for that solution.

103
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:10,780
So in this paper,
they said that it

104
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,400
helped them to have two losses.

105
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:14,860
One is the binary
loss and the other one

106
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:17,860
was the normal
language modeling,

107
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:20,620
next prediction token loss.

108
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:23,820
And the verifier itself, the
architecture of the verifier

109
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:26,620
is also a language model.

110
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:30,620
But also, this language
model has a small scalar head

111
00:06:30,620 --> 00:06:38,140
that outputs the binary
prediction on a per-token basis.

112
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:40,300
So basically, on
a per token we are

113
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,460
saying how close was the
solution to the true labels

114
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:50,610
generated by humans.

115
00:06:50,610 --> 00:06:53,810
And then the tokens in
questions are masked out

116
00:06:53,810 --> 00:06:56,430
because we are not
optimizing for that.

117
00:06:56,430 --> 00:06:58,190
So we generate the question.

118
00:06:58,190 --> 00:07:01,070
We pass in, fill in the
model with the questions,

119
00:07:01,070 --> 00:07:03,190
and we ask it to
generate the solutions.

120
00:07:03,190 --> 00:07:07,890
And we work with
these two losses.

121
00:07:07,890 --> 00:07:09,450
The way they trained
the model was

122
00:07:09,450 --> 00:07:14,010
to fine tune the generator
language model for two epochs,

123
00:07:14,010 --> 00:07:19,570
and then sample 100
completions per question,

124
00:07:19,570 --> 00:07:22,110
label it whether they
were correct or not,

125
00:07:22,110 --> 00:07:27,430
and then train a verifier for
a single epoch on this dataset.

126
00:07:27,430 --> 00:07:32,650
So first, they did fine tune the
language model on that dataset

127
00:07:32,650 --> 00:07:35,170
on a portion of that dataset.

128
00:07:35,170 --> 00:07:40,010
And this step maybe it's like
debatable whether we still

129
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:41,990
need this step or not.

130
00:07:41,990 --> 00:07:43,490
Because language
models are already

131
00:07:43,490 --> 00:07:46,450
very good at instruction
following and understanding

132
00:07:46,450 --> 00:07:47,110
math.

133
00:07:47,110 --> 00:07:49,740
So maybe and for a lot
of newer verifiers,

134
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:53,280
we directly go to the training
the verifier objective

135
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:55,780
without any supervised
fine tuning first.

136
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:02,340
So they did a number
of ablation studies.

137
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:08,600
One was to create this label
of correct or incorrect

138
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:12,440
at the sentence level.

139
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:17,860
So we have a loss that only
concerns at each sentence level.

140
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:21,240
For example, after each period
looks into the previous sentence

141
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:25,400
and looks whether this
is a correct step given

142
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:30,360
of the correct step for
the solution or not,

143
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,740
or they could do
at a token level.

144
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:35,740
So this is very, very
noisy kind of label.

145
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:41,280
Like basically we want
every token to follow.

146
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:45,720
The pattern that leads to
a final correct solution

147
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:49,550
at the end of this generation.

148
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:52,470
And then the way this
is used at test time

149
00:08:52,470 --> 00:08:57,690
is the final solution score is
the score after the last token.

150
00:08:57,690 --> 00:09:03,870
Because if you have a token
label prediction per token,

151
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:06,830
at the end of the day, we
want only one prediction

152
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:09,750
for the entire generation,
whether it's correct or not.

153
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:11,950
And it turns out,
in this case, they

154
00:09:11,950 --> 00:09:13,990
looked it after the last token.

155
00:09:13,990 --> 00:09:14,490
All right.

156
00:09:14,490 --> 00:09:18,290
So we were talking about if you
have a token, a label per token,

157
00:09:18,290 --> 00:09:22,030
at the end of the day, we
want one token, yes or no,

158
00:09:22,030 --> 00:09:24,310
whether this answer
was correct or not.

159
00:09:24,310 --> 00:09:28,190
And this is done in
this case by just

160
00:09:28,190 --> 00:09:30,370
looking at the very last token.

161
00:09:30,370 --> 00:09:33,350
And if that was a correct,
then the entire answer

162
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:36,110
is assumed correct,
and otherwise, it's

163
00:09:36,110 --> 00:09:37,290
assumed incorrect.

164
00:09:37,290 --> 00:09:42,250
So we predict for S1, S2,
SM, we predict these labels.

165
00:09:42,250 --> 00:09:44,420
And then we only look
at the very last label

166
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:50,820
to generate and allocate a
label for the entire solution.

167
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:56,060
So here is-- looking
back at these problems,

168
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:58,380
when we get to the
solution, we are

169
00:09:58,380 --> 00:10:00,860
looking at the
token-level prediction

170
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:02,680
by the train verifier.

171
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:06,060
And if it's green, meaning
the verifier has a high score

172
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:09,700
and the red ones means a
lower score in this case.

173
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:13,880
And in this case, there were
some lower scores in the middle,

174
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:16,800
but towards the end, it
became greener and greener,

175
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:20,320
and this was a correct
prediction at the end.

176
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:23,700
At the end, the very
last stage is a green one

177
00:10:23,700 --> 00:10:26,700
and we assume the actual score
and the verifier predictions

178
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:30,080
are in this case are matching.

179
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:33,700
In this other case, the
last verifier is red.

180
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:36,040
It starts off
well, but it has a.

181
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:45,570
There is something
a mistake is made,

182
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:49,450
which is towards the end
of this generation step

183
00:10:49,450 --> 00:10:52,810
and the actual score is negative
and the verifier prediction

184
00:10:52,810 --> 00:10:54,030
is also negative.

185
00:10:54,030 --> 00:10:58,810
So this is how basically this
verifier is trained for GSM-8K

186
00:10:58,810 --> 00:11:03,170
And then they did some
ablations or comparisons

187
00:11:03,170 --> 00:11:06,850
with a baseline where we
just directly just fine tune

188
00:11:06,850 --> 00:11:10,770
on this dataset fine tune
meaning with supervised fine

189
00:11:10,770 --> 00:11:11,270
tuning.

190
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:15,290
So we just take the base model
and the next token prediction,

191
00:11:15,290 --> 00:11:19,570
or we do this
verification stage where

192
00:11:19,570 --> 00:11:24,150
we generate 100 samples
at each generation,

193
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:26,850
and use the highest
score of the verifier

194
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:30,930
as the true as the delivered
output by our system.

195
00:11:30,930 --> 00:11:37,650
So the idea, what we can
see here is that for both 6B

196
00:11:37,650 --> 00:11:44,360
and 175b models, this one
is a GPT-3 model, I believe.

197
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:48,700
For both of these, verification
seems to work better,

198
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:53,160
especially as the training set
size for the verifier increases.

199
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:57,560
So the verifier model
eventually outperform

200
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:00,881
the fine-tuning-only approach.

201
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:07,420
And vice versa, if you
have smaller data sets--

202
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:12,720
in this case, less than
1,000 for the 175B--

203
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:15,580
the verification is
not helping much.

204
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:21,840
[INAUDIBLE]

205
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:26,860
Sorry, can you speak up?

206
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:37,800
I mean, the test summary of the
6B verification is still much

207
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:44,430
higher than the [INAUDIBLE]
on the smaller [INAUDIBLE].

208
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:48,470
The orange lines still--

209
00:12:48,470 --> 00:12:49,910
Isn't it lower--

210
00:12:49,910 --> 00:12:50,910
OK, I see.

211
00:12:50,910 --> 00:12:52,190
--than 175B?

212
00:12:52,190 --> 00:12:53,670
[OVERLAPPING SPEECH]

213
00:12:53,670 --> 00:12:56,890
OK, let me go here.

214
00:12:56,890 --> 00:13:01,090
So this is another result
which is very interesting.

215
00:13:01,090 --> 00:13:03,310
And this is something
that could potentially

216
00:13:03,310 --> 00:13:07,550
be a research project that
you guys can look into.

217
00:13:07,550 --> 00:13:11,650
So what they did is that
they tested two approaches,

218
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:15,230
one having a larger generator
and smaller verifier,

219
00:13:15,230 --> 00:13:18,770
and two having a smaller
generator and larger verifier.

220
00:13:18,770 --> 00:13:24,150
And what they saw was that
the larger generator helps.

221
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:27,390
The smaller verifier does
better than vice versa.

222
00:13:27,390 --> 00:13:29,710
And to some extent,
this is intuitive,

223
00:13:29,710 --> 00:13:37,670
because if we assume generation
is naturally, or on average,

224
00:13:37,670 --> 00:13:40,260
is a harder task
than verification,

225
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:42,400
then this might make sense.

226
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:47,500
But finding the Pareto
optimal of how these two can

227
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:50,180
be the sizes, with
respect to each other,

228
00:13:50,180 --> 00:13:55,460
would be a very interesting
research question, especially

229
00:13:55,460 --> 00:14:00,280
right now, as the base-model
generators have improved a lot.

230
00:14:00,280 --> 00:14:02,940
And also, we have access
to a lot of verifiers.

231
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:05,620
On Hugging Face,
there's a leaderboard

232
00:14:05,620 --> 00:14:08,820
of verification, verifier
models, or reward models

233
00:14:08,820 --> 00:14:11,300
that can be tested against.

234
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:15,260
So again, this was
the main message

235
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:19,660
from this ablation study.

236
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:26,180
Another result about test-time
scaling that they're showing

237
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:29,860
is that, as they increase
the number of completions

238
00:14:29,860 --> 00:14:32,360
per problem, and then
they use the verifier,

239
00:14:32,360 --> 00:14:37,530
it seems like around 400 or so.

240
00:14:37,530 --> 00:14:39,710
That's where they
get a lot of benefit.

241
00:14:39,710 --> 00:14:42,510
But after that, they're not
getting a lot of benefit.

242
00:14:42,510 --> 00:14:46,210
So if they sample 800
solutions and they

243
00:14:46,210 --> 00:14:49,170
use the verifier against
that, the verifier

244
00:14:49,170 --> 00:14:52,130
fails to track what's
best and what's

245
00:14:52,130 --> 00:14:57,710
not, compared to the true
distribution of the solutions.

246
00:14:57,710 --> 00:15:01,850
So one question for
you is that, do you

247
00:15:01,850 --> 00:15:05,370
remember last time, when we
talked about majority voting?

248
00:15:05,370 --> 00:15:08,850
What was the range that we
