273
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:31,200
was correct or not.

274
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:33,180
My question is that,
in the training time,

275
00:16:33,180 --> 00:16:36,510
you said there's [? over ?]
100 [? fusion. ?]

276
00:16:36,510 --> 00:16:40,890
At test time-- and training
time as well, for the verifier,

277
00:16:40,890 --> 00:16:42,350
but not for the generator.

278
00:16:42,350 --> 00:16:42,850
OK.

279
00:16:42,850 --> 00:16:46,030
For the training times,
all are human [INAUDIBLE]

280
00:16:46,030 --> 00:16:48,770
but the training--

281
00:16:48,770 --> 00:16:52,870
So, for each problem, the human
has once generated an answer.

282
00:16:52,870 --> 00:16:53,930
That's offline.

283
00:16:53,930 --> 00:16:55,190
That's done once.

284
00:16:55,190 --> 00:16:59,510
Then you take the model,
generate 100 solutions

285
00:16:59,510 --> 00:17:03,390
per problem, and you compare
each of the solutions

286
00:17:03,390 --> 00:17:05,390
against the ground
truth, which is

287
00:17:05,390 --> 00:17:07,690
generated only once by humans.

288
00:17:07,690 --> 00:17:09,490
And you see whether
they match or not.

289
00:17:09,490 --> 00:17:13,130
That's how you create the label.

290
00:17:13,130 --> 00:17:13,630
OK.

291
00:17:16,950 --> 00:17:19,869
OK, any questions
from this paper?

292
00:17:19,869 --> 00:17:20,890
Yes?

293
00:17:20,890 --> 00:17:22,258
I have a question.

294
00:17:22,258 --> 00:17:25,069
After 400, it made sense.

295
00:17:25,069 --> 00:17:27,230
The verifier is
increasing results.

296
00:17:27,230 --> 00:17:29,070
But after that,
why does it drop?

297
00:17:29,070 --> 00:17:33,180
Intuitively, why doesn't
it stay constant?

298
00:17:33,180 --> 00:17:37,720
OK, so the question is,
why does it drop after 400?

299
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:39,980
It shouldn't be constant,
because we are not

300
00:17:39,980 --> 00:17:41,280
showing the coverage.

301
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:44,620
We are showing the output
of this entire system, where

302
00:17:44,620 --> 00:17:48,420
the verifier ranks these,
say, at this point, 800

303
00:17:48,420 --> 00:17:50,460
different solutions,
and we are getting

304
00:17:50,460 --> 00:17:52,660
the max score of the verifier.

305
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:56,020
But the precision of
the verifier drops.

306
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:58,920
If two solutions are
too close to each other,

307
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:02,000
but one is correct and
incorrect across 800,

308
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,620
it can't distinguish
between the two

309
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:07,180
as well as it could do for 400.

310
00:18:07,180 --> 00:18:11,220
So that's where we get
