311
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:14,420
And in reality, when
they're outputting--

312
00:18:14,420 --> 00:18:15,920
they created this system.

313
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:19,900
They said that they
stopped at 100 samples

314
00:18:19,900 --> 00:18:23,280
because that's where they
get most of the gains anyway,

315
00:18:23,280 --> 00:18:26,820
and that's where
they're consistent.

316
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:32,230
[INAUDIBLE] it's getting
longer and longer.

317
00:18:32,230 --> 00:18:34,930
Does this mean we
have more answers?

318
00:18:34,930 --> 00:18:38,930
Repeated samples, so parallel.

319
00:18:38,930 --> 00:18:40,390
So, same question.

320
00:18:40,390 --> 00:18:47,210
You ask the model 100
different times to solve that.

321
00:18:47,210 --> 00:18:48,890
Yes?

322
00:18:48,890 --> 00:18:52,850
When they generate a label and
these sort of sentences result,

323
00:18:52,850 --> 00:18:54,690
how do you distinguish?

324
00:18:54,690 --> 00:19:01,510
One could just be a perfect
[INAUDIBLE] matched one-by-one--

325
00:19:01,510 --> 00:19:05,050
Because this is trained
on a larger data set,

326
00:19:05,050 --> 00:19:07,290
and because the
verifier-- and they

327
00:19:07,290 --> 00:19:10,370
consider both sentence-based
and token-based.

328
00:19:10,370 --> 00:19:15,190
It still works because the data
that the trainer-- first of all,

329
00:19:15,190 --> 00:19:18,330
the verifier is a language
model itself, so it knows

330
00:19:18,330 --> 00:19:20,990
a lot about this space itself.

331
00:19:20,990 --> 00:19:24,110
But also, through
this label training,

332
00:19:24,110 --> 00:19:27,800
it has been trained to
be able to distinguish

333
00:19:27,800 --> 00:19:31,880
between correct and incorrect.

334
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:33,000
Yes?

335
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,240
Does it make sense to
do test-time scaling

336
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:37,600
on the verification itself?

337
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:40,680
So the question is-- let me
repeat the question because one

338
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,560
of you asked this before.

339
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:46,740
So the question is, does it make
sense to scale the verifier,

340
00:19:46,740 --> 00:19:48,620
do test-time scaling
on the verifier?

341
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:53,180
Yes, and you're going to see
that in the future slides.

342
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:57,600
Yes?

343
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:00,000
The plot that you showed
verification versus

344
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:04,760
fine-tuning-- you showed that
before, around 1,000 steps,

345
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:06,300
1,000 training size, right?

346
00:20:06,300 --> 00:20:10,800
The fine-tuning actually does
better than the verifier.

347
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:15,760
Does that happen again if you
scale it to a higher training--

348
00:20:15,760 --> 00:20:19,140
Do you mean the fine-tuning
versus verification?

349
00:20:19,140 --> 00:20:20,600
Do they cross each other--

350
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:22,677
Again, yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

351
00:20:28,350 --> 00:20:30,310
If you were to actually--

352
00:20:30,310 --> 00:20:36,310
if you do need that much
data, for example, for a task,

353
00:20:36,310 --> 00:20:37,910
do you ever--

354
00:20:37,910 --> 00:20:39,270
I mean, I think that you would.

355
00:20:39,270 --> 00:20:45,710
If you have a ton of data,
a whole lot of data, then

356
00:20:45,710 --> 00:20:48,230
fine-tuning and
verification-- probably,

357
00:20:48,230 --> 00:20:49,470
they're going to be the same.

358
00:20:49,470 --> 00:20:52,270
They'll converge
to the same thing.

359
00:20:52,270 --> 00:20:56,590
But the beauty of a verifier
is that you are not fine-tuning

360
00:20:56,590 --> 00:20:58,010
your base language model.

361
00:20:58,010 --> 00:21:02,390
You're not making it really
custom to one data set or one

362
00:21:02,390 --> 00:21:03,710
specific task.

363
00:21:03,710 --> 00:21:06,270
So that base model,
the generator model,

364
00:21:06,270 --> 00:21:08,110
remains general.

365
00:21:08,110 --> 00:21:09,990
And then, now, you
have a verifier

366
00:21:09,990 --> 00:21:15,770
that you can lead
the base model.

367
00:21:18,770 --> 00:21:25,940
Now let's go through the second
paper, another paper by OpenAI.

368
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:28,160
Let's verify, step by step.

369
00:21:32,180 --> 00:21:38,820
And so this came about a couple
years, less than two years,

370
00:21:38,820 --> 00:21:40,820
after that first paper.

371
00:21:40,820 --> 00:21:43,640
Again, LLMs have had
the same challenges.

372
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:46,340
They hallucinated.

373
00:21:46,340 --> 00:21:49,140
If they're solving a
problem step by step,

374
00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:51,660
and they make a
step in a single--

375
00:21:51,660 --> 00:21:56,620
a misstep early on-- it can
derail the entire answer, right?

376
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:59,740
So the solution that
they were proposing here

377
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:04,460
is that they consider these
two kind of approaches

378
00:22:04,460 --> 00:22:05,800
to reward modeling.

379
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:09,820
One is outcome-based
reward model,

380
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:12,320
and the other is
process-based reward model.

381
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:15,420
So the outcome-based
one, like we saw earlier,

382
00:22:15,420 --> 00:22:18,500
in the previous paper--
it generates a reward

383
00:22:18,500 --> 00:22:20,520
to the entire solution.

384
00:22:20,520 --> 00:22:24,450
It's about the correctness
of the solution,

385
00:22:24,450 --> 00:22:28,810
of the entire solution,
whereas the process one assigns

386
00:22:28,810 --> 00:22:32,550
a different reward per step
of the optimization problem.

387
00:22:36,450 --> 00:22:40,170
And so let's take a look
exactly how this training can

388
00:22:40,170 --> 00:22:42,670
be done for the ORM versus PRM.

389
00:22:42,670 --> 00:22:44,070
So we have a math problem.

390
00:22:44,070 --> 00:22:48,930
The generator-- instead of
generating 100 solutions,

391
00:22:48,930 --> 00:22:52,430
right now, we are only
looking into one solution.

392
00:22:52,430 --> 00:22:54,470
And it has a bunch
of steps-- step 1,

393
00:22:54,470 --> 00:22:59,290
2, and all the way until
we get to the final answer.

394
00:22:59,290 --> 00:23:01,050
And then we have
the ground truth,

395
00:23:01,050 --> 00:23:05,450
so we can know whether the
final answer is correct

396
00:23:05,450 --> 00:23:08,490
or not because we can match the
final answer against the ground

397
00:23:08,490 --> 00:23:10,530
truth.

398
00:23:10,530 --> 00:23:14,370
Now, with ORM, we are done
there because that's how we

399
00:23:14,370 --> 00:23:17,410
assign the label for the model.

400
00:23:17,410 --> 00:23:22,120
But for PRM, the way
they did it in this paper

401
00:23:22,120 --> 00:23:24,600
was that they looked into--

402
00:23:24,600 --> 00:23:28,960
they had human annotators
go through the steps

403
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:35,360
of the generations by the model
and assign a score to each step.

404
00:23:35,360 --> 00:23:37,460
So step 1 in this
case was correct.

405
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:38,580
Step 2 was correct.

406
00:23:38,580 --> 00:23:43,340
And probably because, maybe,
all of these steps were correct,

407
00:23:43,340 --> 00:23:46,880
that's why it led to the
final, correct answer.

408
00:23:46,880 --> 00:23:50,680
But basically, they created
this entire data set

409
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:58,920
of human-annotated, stepwise,
correct or incorrect labels.

410
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:02,960
And the final reward
for this stepwise now,

411
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:05,240
again, in this case, could be--

412
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:08,320
what they propose is
that can be calculated

413
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:13,340
as the product of the stepwise
rewards, per step of the model.

414
00:24:13,340 --> 00:24:15,200
So they basically
train the model

415
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,960
against these
stepwise annotations,

416
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:19,340
and then they use that.

417
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:21,230
They create steps.

418
00:24:21,230 --> 00:24:24,790
You can even ask the model
to generate the answers

419
00:24:24,790 --> 00:24:26,230
to a solution step by step.

420
00:24:26,230 --> 00:24:27,950
So the steps are clear.

421
00:24:27,950 --> 00:24:30,410
They run this PRM against
each of those steps.

422
00:24:30,410 --> 00:24:31,210
They get a score.

423
00:24:31,210 --> 00:24:33,210
They multiply them
with each other.

424
00:24:33,210 --> 00:24:39,070
They get a range of the
quality of the answer.

425
00:24:39,070 --> 00:24:43,150
And based on that, they
can decide whether they

426
00:24:43,150 --> 00:24:47,230
output the answer or try again.

427
00:24:47,230 --> 00:24:50,510
So this is called
process supervision.

428
00:24:50,510 --> 00:24:53,050
Obviously, there is
this credit assignment,

429
00:24:53,050 --> 00:24:55,390
and then it's the
more precise way

430
00:24:55,390 --> 00:24:58,910
of collecting data
and assigning labels

431
00:24:58,910 --> 00:25:00,910
to different steps
of the problem,

432
00:25:00,910 --> 00:25:06,590
rather than just looking
at the output answer.

433
00:25:06,590 --> 00:25:09,250
A property here that's
very important-- actually,

434
00:25:09,250 --> 00:25:11,190
this is a really--

435
00:25:11,190 --> 00:25:14,750
it could be a pitfall
for test-time scaling,

436
00:25:14,750 --> 00:25:17,470
is that, with
process supervision,

437
00:25:17,470 --> 00:25:19,940
we can manage the
false positives

438
00:25:19,940 --> 00:25:25,060
way better than we can do
with just outcome supervision.

439
00:25:25,060 --> 00:25:25,740
Why?

440
00:25:25,740 --> 00:25:28,160
Because model might hallucinate.

441
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:29,680
And this happens, surprisingly.

442
00:25:29,680 --> 00:25:33,620
Model can hallucinate and get
to a final, correct answer

443
00:25:33,620 --> 00:25:37,500
while the process for
it is really wrong,

444
00:25:37,500 --> 00:25:39,280
whereas, with
process supervision,

445
00:25:39,280 --> 00:25:43,380
because we are supervising,
we are seeing all the steps,

446
00:25:43,380 --> 00:25:46,900
and we have a score for it,
it's less likely that we

447
00:25:46,900 --> 00:25:51,600
get into the mode where
the steps are wrong,

448
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:56,500
but the final answer is correct.

449
00:25:56,500 --> 00:25:59,740
Also, it encourages
interpretable reasoning

450
00:25:59,740 --> 00:26:04,700
and human-endorsed
process of solutions.

451
00:26:04,700 --> 00:26:06,780
It's human-endorsed,
again, because humans

452
00:26:06,780 --> 00:26:09,900
have created the labels.

453
00:26:09,900 --> 00:26:13,460
So this paper produced
this data set, PRM800K.

454
00:26:16,370 --> 00:26:21,930
It's an open-source data set
of 800k step-level labels.

455
00:26:25,890 --> 00:26:30,150
So the way they did this
was a generator model.

456
00:26:30,150 --> 00:26:32,090
They took a language
model, and they

457
00:26:32,090 --> 00:26:34,730
generate a large number
of samples per problem

458
00:26:34,730 --> 00:26:37,390
in a stepwise manner.

459
00:26:37,390 --> 00:26:39,490
And then they also
did this trick

460
00:26:39,490 --> 00:26:44,090
of-- because they wanted to
collect higher-quality stepwise

461
00:26:44,090 --> 00:26:47,770
labels, they took
into what they call

462
00:26:47,770 --> 00:26:53,090
convincing wrong
kind of answers,

463
00:26:53,090 --> 00:26:59,330
meaning that they gave higher
priority to samples where

464
00:26:59,330 --> 00:27:05,810
the final answers were correct,
but the intermediate steps

465
00:27:05,810 --> 00:27:06,950
were incorrect.

466
00:27:09,890 --> 00:27:15,560
And this enabled them to be
2.6 times more data-efficient

467
00:27:15,560 --> 00:27:18,960
than just randomly
selecting these samples

468
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:20,600
and give it to humans.

469
00:27:20,600 --> 00:27:23,600
So they did it iteratively.

470
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:29,080
You can have a PRM by just
asking an LLM to judge a step,

471
00:27:29,080 --> 00:27:29,580
right?

472
00:27:29,580 --> 00:27:31,560
You can have that.

473
00:27:31,560 --> 00:27:36,160
So they iteratively upgraded
the PRM by collecting this data

474
00:27:36,160 --> 00:27:40,480
and then training the PRM
for it and then increasing

475
00:27:40,480 --> 00:27:44,140
the quality of the data.

476
00:27:44,140 --> 00:27:47,280
And at each step, the
label that was collected

477
00:27:47,280 --> 00:27:49,540
was either positive or
negative or neutral.

478
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:53,400
For example, let's take
a look at this problem.

479
00:27:53,400 --> 00:27:59,960
The denominator of a fraction
is 7 less than 3 times

480
00:27:59,960 --> 00:28:01,020
the numerator.

481
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:03,760
If the fraction is
equivalent to 2 over 5,

482
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:06,540
what is the numerator
of the fraction?

483
00:28:06,540 --> 00:28:08,400
And then there are these steps.

484
00:28:08,400 --> 00:28:12,020
And the first step was labeled
correct, second was correct,

485
00:28:12,020 --> 00:28:14,870
and all the way to the
fifth step was correct.

486
00:28:14,870 --> 00:28:18,390
But this last step, obviously,
the model failed to do the math.

487
00:28:18,390 --> 00:28:21,283
x should be 14, but it's 7.

488
00:28:21,283 --> 00:28:22,950
So that was labeled
incorrect, so that's

489
00:28:22,950 --> 00:28:26,090
how they collected the labels.

490
00:28:29,990 --> 00:28:31,330
Here is the process.

491
00:28:31,330 --> 00:28:35,510
So they took GPT-4
as their base model.

492
00:28:35,510 --> 00:28:40,030
They still fine-tuned GPT-4
for both the ORM and PRM

493
00:28:40,030 --> 00:28:45,470
training on this data set first,
so the supervised fine-tuning

494
00:28:45,470 --> 00:28:46,210
first.

495
00:28:49,230 --> 00:28:52,830
And for ORM, of
course, they just

496
00:28:52,830 --> 00:28:56,410
have the pairs of generated
samples and final correctness.

497
00:28:56,410 --> 00:28:59,990
But for PRM, they had
this generator generate

498
00:28:59,990 --> 00:29:04,790
the sample and then the
stepwise correctness label.

499
00:29:04,790 --> 00:29:07,710
For ORM, the final
score was the score

500
00:29:07,710 --> 00:29:09,890
of the final token
in the completion.

501
00:29:09,890 --> 00:29:14,580
Again, they again went to the
token-level approach for ORM,

502
00:29:14,580 --> 00:29:21,340
but for PRM, it was the product
of probabilities for every step.

503
00:29:21,340 --> 00:29:24,580
Now let's take a look at
some of the solutions.

504
00:29:24,580 --> 00:29:27,340
They were showing
that PRM outperforms

505
00:29:27,340 --> 00:29:29,400
ORM and majority voting.

506
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:33,740
So again, here, n is the number
of solutions per problem.

507
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:36,620
Majority voting,
again, fails after,

508
00:29:36,620 --> 00:29:39,740
in this case, 100 or so samples.

509
00:29:39,740 --> 00:29:42,980
The blue line is the ORM
model, and the orange one

510
00:29:42,980 --> 00:29:46,760
is the PRM approach
that is doing better.

511
00:29:49,300 --> 00:29:52,020
But they noticed that PRM
detects correct solutions

512
00:29:52,020 --> 00:29:57,240
for some of the very rare
occurrences of a correct answer,

513
00:29:57,240 --> 00:30:00,580
some problems with less
than 5% correct answers

514
00:30:00,580 --> 00:30:02,640
in the distribution of samples.

515
00:30:06,460 --> 00:30:09,660
They also showed the
benefit of larger data in

516
00:30:09,660 --> 00:30:16,050
and this active learning
approach that they had, again.

517
00:30:16,050 --> 00:30:21,250
And the other observation
that they were showing

518
00:30:21,250 --> 00:30:25,150
