618
00:35:37,580 --> 00:35:40,560
So it doesn't really
encourage the thinking process

619
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:43,120
of going to step-by-step.

620
00:35:43,120 --> 00:35:44,960
It doesn't, no.

621
00:35:44,960 --> 00:35:45,860
No, it doesn't.

622
00:35:45,860 --> 00:35:49,320
But you can prompt the model
to do this because you're not

623
00:35:49,320 --> 00:35:50,460
changing the model.

624
00:35:50,460 --> 00:35:54,340
You're not changing
your generator.

625
00:35:54,340 --> 00:35:57,040
That failure mode could
be when you were fine-tune

626
00:35:57,040 --> 00:35:58,380
your generator model.

627
00:35:58,380 --> 00:36:02,270
Then the model stops
reasoning and just generates

628
00:36:02,270 --> 00:36:04,650
a final answer that
the PRM likes, right?

629
00:36:04,650 --> 00:36:07,170
But you're not touching
your generator.

630
00:36:07,170 --> 00:36:10,190
The generators can still be
prompted to do reasoning,

631
00:36:10,190 --> 00:36:13,650
create these steps, and then
the verifier just scores them.

632
00:36:16,710 --> 00:36:20,710
Let's say we want to use
the PRM to train the--

633
00:36:20,710 --> 00:36:22,730
Yeah, then you
have to be careful.

634
00:36:22,730 --> 00:36:25,790
Then you have to make sure
that the chain of thought

635
00:36:25,790 --> 00:36:26,570
makes sense.

636
00:36:26,570 --> 00:36:31,870
And again, the way
you train your PRM

637
00:36:31,870 --> 00:36:37,550
can also be aware of that
because your PRM could be--

638
00:36:37,550 --> 00:36:41,970
these are human-generated
labels in this case, right?

639
00:36:41,970 --> 00:36:46,050
If we had skipped all of this,
and then we would go to x 14,

640
00:36:46,050 --> 00:36:49,010
probably, the human says,
this doesn't make sense.

641
00:36:49,010 --> 00:36:54,350
This step is lower, because this
step is skipping a few reasoning

642
00:36:54,350 --> 00:36:56,550
steps.

643
00:36:56,550 --> 00:36:57,490
Does that make sense?

644
00:36:57,490 --> 00:37:00,870
So these labels are
created by humans here.

645
00:37:00,870 --> 00:37:03,750
[INAUDIBLE]

646
00:37:05,190 --> 00:37:08,050
So if the labels are not
generated by humans--

647
00:37:08,050 --> 00:37:10,270
and we are going to see that
in the next paper-- yes,

648
00:37:10,270 --> 00:37:12,750
that is definitely a
caveat and something

649
00:37:12,750 --> 00:37:14,210
that you can make sure.

650
00:37:14,210 --> 00:37:16,670
But there are ways to
handle that as well.

651
00:37:16,670 --> 00:37:19,650
So for now, let's assume
these are human labels,

652
00:37:19,650 --> 00:37:21,630
so the processes
are labeled in a way

653
00:37:21,630 --> 00:37:23,710
that they make sense as well.

654
00:37:23,710 --> 00:37:27,550
We have so many things to
encourage these generation

655
00:37:27,550 --> 00:37:28,570
to make sense.

656
00:37:28,570 --> 00:37:33,490
First of all, the generator
should generate the steps.

657
00:37:33,490 --> 00:37:36,430
The PRM is trained
on human annotations

658
00:37:36,430 --> 00:37:39,030
that they also make sure
that this step makes

659
00:37:39,030 --> 00:37:43,310
sense, given the prior steps.

660
00:37:43,310 --> 00:37:45,010
Any other questions?

661
00:37:48,390 --> 00:37:53,470
OK, let's go through the
next paper, Math-Shepherd.

662
00:37:53,470 --> 00:37:56,230
Verify and reinforce
LLMs step-by-step

663
00:37:56,230 --> 00:37:57,720
without human annotations.

664
00:37:57,720 --> 00:38:01,340
So we're getting to,
what if we didn't

665
00:38:01,340 --> 00:38:06,960
want to collect that many
annotations from humans?

666
00:38:06,960 --> 00:38:14,900
So again, motivation is that
having stronger verifiers

667
00:38:14,900 --> 00:38:18,140
shows the potential to really
improve model's reasoning

668
00:38:18,140 --> 00:38:22,100
behavior, especially if
you do test-time scaling.

669
00:38:22,100 --> 00:38:25,280
PRMs have shown more
potential than ORMs,

670
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:28,180
but they require a
lot of data, and it's

671
00:38:28,180 --> 00:38:30,000
hard to collect these data.

672
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:37,740
So can we automate the process
of label collection for PRMs?

673
00:38:37,740 --> 00:38:41,440
So this paper talks about
two kind of approaches.

674
00:38:41,440 --> 00:38:50,980
One is automatic annotation,
and also brings the reward model

675
00:38:50,980 --> 00:38:55,930
into the loop of generator
optimization via a reinforcement

676
00:38:55,930 --> 00:38:58,610
learning approach, which--

677
00:38:58,610 --> 00:39:00,810
we are going to
briefly mention that.

678
00:39:00,810 --> 00:39:06,690
So, in this case,
the way they try

679
00:39:06,690 --> 00:39:11,690
to go about this automatic
annotation of steps

680
00:39:11,690 --> 00:39:16,810
was that they define the
quality of the reasons step

681
00:39:16,810 --> 00:39:19,050
as the potential of
this step to reach

682
00:39:19,050 --> 00:39:21,170
to the final, correct answer.

683
00:39:21,170 --> 00:39:24,290
So let's say we are
at a given step.

684
00:39:24,290 --> 00:39:28,650
We sample N completions
from that given step

685
00:39:28,650 --> 00:39:32,970
and see the potential
for a correct solution

686
00:39:32,970 --> 00:39:34,170
at the very end.

687
00:39:34,170 --> 00:39:37,110
And they introduce two
ways of doing this.

688
00:39:37,110 --> 00:39:46,550
One is the hard estimate, where
a step is annotated as a success

689
00:39:46,550 --> 00:39:51,890
if any of those n
generations after that step

690
00:39:51,890 --> 00:39:55,040
gets to a final, correct answer.

691
00:39:55,040 --> 00:39:58,350
The soft estimate
measures the frequency,

692
00:39:58,350 --> 00:40:02,320
like what portion of the
generations after a current step

693
00:40:02,320 --> 00:40:04,320
reached the final,
correct answer?

694
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:07,080
So here is how it works.

695
00:40:07,080 --> 00:40:11,660
Say here is the problem, and
this is our current state.

696
00:40:11,660 --> 00:40:17,060
Instead of just sampling one
step, N is 3 in this case,

697
00:40:17,060 --> 00:40:18,880
so we sample three steps.

698
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:23,400
And then we continue, and
we measure the frequency.

699
00:40:23,400 --> 00:40:27,280
In the soft estimate, we measure
the frequency of correct answer

700
00:40:27,280 --> 00:40:28,220
at the very end.

701
00:40:28,220 --> 00:40:34,860
In hard estimate, we see whether
any of these are correct or not.

702
00:40:34,860 --> 00:40:37,760
So hard estimate
would be 1 for step 1.

703
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:40,660
Soft estimate would
be 2/3 for step 1.

704
00:40:43,520 --> 00:40:53,910
Anyone can name a challenge or
a drawback of this approach?

705
00:40:53,910 --> 00:40:59,990
So basically, this
soft or hard estimation

706
00:40:59,990 --> 00:41:03,130
is replacing the
human annotations.

707
00:41:03,130 --> 00:41:05,510
So we're getting
a score per step

708
00:41:05,510 --> 00:41:08,510
by just rolling out more
samples from that step

709
00:41:08,510 --> 00:41:12,190
and seeing whether we reach
to the final, correct answer

710
00:41:12,190 --> 00:41:13,870
or not, and assume
that we do have

711
00:41:13,870 --> 00:41:16,050
final labels for these problems.

712
00:41:21,150 --> 00:41:22,710
Yes?

713
00:41:22,710 --> 00:41:28,390
So we-- for unpredictable
paths [INAUDIBLE]

714
00:41:28,390 --> 00:41:35,790
lower when the unpredictable
path could be built [INAUDIBLE].

715
00:41:35,790 --> 00:41:36,990
Sorry, speak up again.

716
00:41:36,990 --> 00:41:38,910
If it's unpredictable--

717
00:41:38,910 --> 00:41:42,150
Or unusual approaches
to solving a problem.

718
00:41:42,150 --> 00:41:44,810
It could be scored
as low or something,

719
00:41:44,810 --> 00:41:48,310
while that might be the way to
be able to solve that problem.

720
00:41:48,310 --> 00:41:50,180
It might be a
really hard problem.

721
00:41:50,180 --> 00:41:54,640
Now likely to lose that
path to that solution.

722
00:41:54,640 --> 00:41:55,560
That is correct.

723
00:41:55,560 --> 00:41:58,960
So basically, say N is 3 here.

724
00:41:58,960 --> 00:42:02,660
Maybe we needed N
equal 100 to see

725
00:42:02,660 --> 00:42:09,140
that final, correct
trajectory to score this step,

726
00:42:09,140 --> 00:42:12,000
but we won't see that
if we just look into 3,

727
00:42:12,000 --> 00:42:15,780
and then we give a score of 0.

728
00:42:15,780 --> 00:42:16,920
Anything else?

729
00:42:23,500 --> 00:42:24,437
Yes?

730
00:42:24,437 --> 00:42:26,020
I guess this is
similar, but if you're

731
00:42:26,020 --> 00:42:28,100
dealing with a hard
question, if you're

732
00:42:28,100 --> 00:42:31,000
taking the approach of
generating a lot of samples,

733
00:42:31,000 --> 00:42:33,460
a lot of the samples can
just not be correct or not

734
00:42:33,460 --> 00:42:34,800
be the correct way to do it.

735
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:36,460
So--

736
00:42:36,460 --> 00:42:42,220
You don't get any signal for
hard problems in this case.

737
00:42:42,220 --> 00:42:44,244
That's right.

738
00:42:44,244 --> 00:42:48,490
[INAUDIBLE]

739
00:42:48,490 --> 00:42:49,550
Exactly.

740
00:42:49,550 --> 00:42:51,570
I think this is probably--

741
00:42:51,570 --> 00:42:54,250
these two that you
all mentioned here--

742
00:42:54,250 --> 00:42:56,410
for hard problems,
you don't get signal.

743
00:42:56,410 --> 00:43:00,370
The other one is, what if there
are wrong steps in the middle,

744
00:43:00,370 --> 00:43:03,730
and we got to the
final, correct solution?

745
00:43:03,730 --> 00:43:08,130
We still label those
steps as correct.

746
00:43:08,130 --> 00:43:10,850
So the hope is that, if
you have more samples,

747
00:43:10,850 --> 00:43:15,990
it shows the wrongness in
one of these trajectories,

748
00:43:15,990 --> 00:43:17,910
but it's not guaranteed.

749
00:43:24,974 --> 00:43:27,750
So here's the
verification process.

750
00:43:27,750 --> 00:43:32,450
You sample N again, sample
N candidate solutions,

751
00:43:32,450 --> 00:43:38,250
score them using the PRM, and
you select the highest PRM score

752
00:43:38,250 --> 00:43:42,230
across the generated samples.

753
00:43:42,230 --> 00:43:47,680
They also took this PRM and
trained the model with this PRM

754
00:43:47,680 --> 00:43:51,800
to encourage the model to
generate steps that are scored

755
00:43:51,800 --> 00:43:53,340
higher and higher by PRM.

756
00:43:53,340 --> 00:43:58,800
So PRM basically served as
the reward model for this.

757
00:43:58,800 --> 00:44:02,340
So they looked into the hard
versus soft annotations,

758
00:44:02,340 --> 00:44:09,360
and although the loss function
as they increased N would be--

759
00:44:09,360 --> 00:44:14,520
it seemed like the soft
annotations were doing better.

760
00:44:14,520 --> 00:44:21,880
But somehow what they
observed with the N equal 4,

761
00:44:21,880 --> 00:44:24,960
the depth of the
trajectory completions

762
00:44:24,960 --> 00:44:28,920
to collect these labels--
that's where they get their best

763
00:44:28,920 --> 00:44:31,000
results anyway.

764
00:44:31,000 --> 00:44:32,880
So it really didn't
matter whether they

765
00:44:32,880 --> 00:44:35,060
used the hard estimate
or soft estimate,

766
00:44:35,060 --> 00:44:37,740
and they went with the hard
estimate at the very end

767
00:44:37,740 --> 00:44:42,320
because it was just
easier to measure that.

768
00:44:42,320 --> 00:44:51,470
So here is another
comparison with baseline.

769
00:44:51,470 --> 00:44:53,750
"SC" is self-consistency.

770
00:44:53,750 --> 00:44:56,050
It's another name
for majority voting.

771
00:44:56,050 --> 00:44:59,190
Which solution was
most consistent?

772
00:44:59,190 --> 00:45:01,510
That's the red one.

773
00:45:01,510 --> 00:45:06,070
ORM is the blue one, and the
SHEPHERD is this PRM approach,

774
00:45:06,070 --> 00:45:07,930
is the green one.

775
00:45:07,930 --> 00:45:10,590
And the interesting
part here was that there

776
00:45:10,590 --> 00:45:12,250
was no human annotation.

777
00:45:12,250 --> 00:45:17,050
All the annotations
were modeled, generated,

778
00:45:17,050 --> 00:45:21,910
or the mechanism
that they introduced.

779
00:45:21,910 --> 00:45:24,850
Here, they also
compare with PRM800K,

780
00:45:24,850 --> 00:45:30,390
which was the verifier in the
previous paper that was trained.

781
00:45:30,390 --> 00:45:33,010
And it outperformed
that as well on MATH,

782
00:45:33,010 --> 00:45:37,110
which is like a much harder
set of-- this data set

783
00:45:37,110 --> 00:45:40,710
is more difficult than
GSM8K, and they were getting

784
00:45:40,710 --> 00:45:43,410
an even higher delta in there.

785
00:45:46,060 --> 00:45:51,820
They also compared these
results with different types

786
00:45:51,820 --> 00:45:59,480
of verifiers again across
GSM8K and MATH500 problems.

787
00:45:59,480 --> 00:46:02,260
And you can see that the
difference between these models

788
00:46:02,260 --> 00:46:05,180
and these approaches--

789
00:46:05,180 --> 00:46:10,940
and there is a
notable difference

790
00:46:10,940 --> 00:46:13,860
between these models
and the baselines,

791
00:46:13,860 --> 00:46:18,660
which is self-consistency
approaches.

792
00:46:18,660 --> 00:46:20,620
And this is true across
different models--

793
00:46:20,620 --> 00:46:26,180
LLaMA-70B, LLema-34B,
and DeepSeek-67B.

794
00:46:29,100 --> 00:46:31,800
They also showed that
they can get even better.

795
00:46:31,800 --> 00:46:33,260
They can make the
model even better

796
00:46:33,260 --> 00:46:37,480
if they RL the model against
the PRMs that they trained.

797
00:46:37,480 --> 00:46:39,620
So, in this case,
the Math-Shepherd,

798
00:46:39,620 --> 00:46:46,450
on their approach-- basically,
taking Mistral-7B, this model,

799
00:46:46,450 --> 00:46:50,130
and then they PPO
it against this PRM.

800
00:46:50,130 --> 00:46:53,010
They're getting
much higher results,

801
00:46:53,010 --> 00:46:59,610
compared to doing this
RL with just an ORM.

802
00:46:59,610 --> 00:47:02,830
The delta here is slightly
less, I would say,

803
00:47:02,830 --> 00:47:07,930
than just the test-time scaling,
but this is also another way

804
00:47:07,930 --> 00:47:09,890
for the models to self-improve.

805
00:47:09,890 --> 00:47:14,370
You generate the annotation
by the model itself.

806
00:47:14,370 --> 00:47:17,130
You train the PRM
and then use that PRM

807
00:47:17,130 --> 00:47:20,730
to improve the generator model
as well, both at test time

808
00:47:20,730 --> 00:47:22,830
and with this RL fine-tuning.

809
00:47:22,830 --> 00:47:26,170
So it's multiple levels
of using the model itself

810
00:47:26,170 --> 00:47:31,150
to generate data and RL
reward-signal for itself.

811
00:47:38,290 --> 00:47:43,180
And then they can do RL and
PPO and do verification on top,

812
00:47:43,180 --> 00:47:44,980
and they get even
better results,

813
00:47:44,980 --> 00:47:51,360
but it seems their optimizations
are plateauing at some point.

814
00:47:51,360 --> 00:47:53,460
Any questions from this paper?

815
00:47:56,280 --> 00:47:56,840
Yes?

816
00:47:56,840 --> 00:47:59,360
Are there ways to
build PRMs that

817
00:47:59,360 --> 00:48:02,520
incentivize self-error
correction, which I imagine

818
00:48:02,520 --> 00:48:03,940
would be a desirable thing?

819
00:48:03,940 --> 00:48:07,260
But if we're only rewarding
each step being correct,

820
00:48:07,260 --> 00:48:09,700
we don't necessarily
end up reinforcing--

821
00:48:09,700 --> 00:48:13,840
you get a lot of incorrect
steps back and stuff like that?

822
00:48:13,840 --> 00:48:18,360
So encourage PRMs to do
more self-correction?

823
00:48:18,360 --> 00:48:19,860
Yeah.

824
00:48:19,860 --> 00:48:20,620
Yes, absolutely.

825
00:48:20,620 --> 00:48:24,400
For example, one of the
methods that can be done

826
00:48:24,400 --> 00:48:27,680
is that you have a
rubric, or a set of rules,

827
00:48:27,680 --> 00:48:32,520
that you give the model
as your scoring step.

828
00:48:32,520 --> 00:48:35,840
So the model can check the
step against this rubric

829
00:48:35,840 --> 00:48:38,550
that you have designed
for this data set,

830
00:48:38,550 --> 00:48:41,790
and that can be
used as extra signal

831
00:48:41,790 --> 00:48:45,190
whether this step
makes sense or not.

832
00:48:45,190 --> 00:48:49,710
For example, use a
calculator in that case,

833
00:48:49,710 --> 00:48:51,550
in the equation
that we are seeing.

834
00:48:51,550 --> 00:48:54,270
If the model could
do tool use or use

835
00:48:54,270 --> 00:48:57,790
a calculator in that case,
it could solve that problem

836
00:48:57,790 --> 00:48:58,890
easily--

837
00:48:58,890 --> 00:49:02,790
could, for example, use SymPy.

838
00:49:02,790 --> 00:49:07,270
So there are ways to bring more
supervision and more signal

839
00:49:07,270 --> 00:49:11,630
and enable the model to use
tools and test-time scaling

840
00:49:11,630 --> 00:49:14,190
and agentic approaches to
improve the verification

841
00:49:14,190 --> 00:49:15,050
process.

842
00:49:19,630 --> 00:49:20,190
Yes?

843
00:49:20,190 --> 00:49:23,870
[INAUDIBLE] verifiers, do
they control the amount

844
00:49:23,870 --> 00:49:25,310
of test-time compute?

845
00:49:29,910 --> 00:49:31,190
I believe so.

846
00:49:31,190 --> 00:49:33,830
You mean "test time" as when
they're using the verifier,

847
00:49:33,830 --> 00:49:37,060
like a baseline verifier,
versus themselves?

848
00:49:37,060 --> 00:49:40,460
I'm actually not sure if
they do that when they're

849
00:49:40,460 --> 00:49:42,600
doing process versus
ORM, because, again,

850
00:49:42,600 --> 00:49:46,580
that's really hard to do.

851
00:49:46,580 --> 00:49:50,980
[INAUDIBLE] because
you're scaling things

852
00:49:50,980 --> 00:49:52,040
at different rates.

853
00:49:52,040 --> 00:49:55,140
Yeah, I think that could
actually be a question

854
00:49:55,140 --> 00:49:56,620
that you could investigate.

855
00:49:56,620 --> 00:49:58,980
You can sample more
in the generator.

856
00:49:58,980 --> 00:50:01,420
You could sample
more in the verifier.

857
00:50:01,420 --> 00:50:04,580
If you're controlling
for a fixed compute,

858
00:50:04,580 --> 00:50:07,020
how do you allocate that?

859
00:50:07,020 --> 00:50:10,460
So one of the challenges here is
that, usually, the ORM and PRM

860
00:50:10,460 --> 00:50:12,580
should come from
the same data for us

861
00:50:12,580 --> 00:50:16,200
to do a good
apples-to-apples comparison,

862
00:50:16,200 --> 00:50:20,560
because if you do train a PRM,
and someone else train an ORM,

863
00:50:20,560 --> 00:50:21,960
it makes things a bit harder.

864
00:50:21,960 --> 00:50:26,300
The training data
also matters a lot.

865
00:50:26,300 --> 00:50:27,820
Any other questions?

866
00:50:27,820 --> 00:50:29,180
Yes?

867
00:50:29,180 --> 00:50:31,460
Do you could back to
the previous slide?

868
00:50:31,460 --> 00:50:33,060
Yeah, I was just-- oh, no.

869
00:50:33,060 --> 00:50:34,940
Basically, for this
one, I was just curious

870
00:50:34,940 --> 00:50:38,250
as to why, after
using RL, they stuck

871
00:50:38,250 --> 00:50:41,650
with evaluating performances
with greedy decoding.

872
00:50:41,650 --> 00:50:45,330
I was just curious whether or
not raising the temperature

873
00:50:45,330 --> 00:50:47,330
would have made a
difference, or that

874
00:50:47,330 --> 00:50:51,310
was the only way they could
have seen the smallest delta.

875
00:50:55,310 --> 00:50:57,950
So the question is why they
stopped greedy decoding,

876
00:50:57,950 --> 00:51:01,328
why they didn't do temperature
changes and test-time scaling

877
00:51:01,328 --> 00:51:01,870
again, right?

878
00:51:01,870 --> 00:51:04,370
I think that's
what this slide is.

879
00:51:04,370 --> 00:51:10,330
So then, in this
case, the verification

880
00:51:10,330 --> 00:51:12,710
is done against 256 outputs.

881
00:51:12,710 --> 00:51:16,190
So the previous one is
no verification, right?

882
00:51:16,190 --> 00:51:18,850
The verifier is just using
the RL training here,

883
00:51:18,850 --> 00:51:20,310
just at test time as well.

884
00:51:25,090 --> 00:51:27,150
I think it's something
that they didn't do,

885
00:51:27,150 --> 00:51:28,608
and it would be
interesting to see.

886
00:51:28,608 --> 00:51:33,320
What if they repeat the
process again, generate

887
00:51:33,320 --> 00:51:37,140
another set of labels,
and train a verifier,

888
00:51:37,140 --> 00:51:40,840
but starting from
this RL-PPO'd model?

889
00:51:47,800 --> 00:51:51,720
OK, let's get to the
last paper, shrinking

890
00:51:51,720 --> 00:51:55,460
the generation-verification
gap with weak verifiers.

891
00:51:55,460 --> 00:52:05,680
So this is a paper from Stanford
that came out a couple months

892
00:52:05,680 --> 00:52:07,320
ago at this point.

893
00:52:07,320 --> 00:52:11,360
So it's a NeurIPS 2025 paper.

894
00:52:11,360 --> 00:52:18,640
So the motivation,
again, is the same.

895
00:52:18,640 --> 00:52:21,600
We still have a
generation-verification gap,

896
00:52:21,600 --> 00:52:25,280
but the approach here
was a bit different.

897
00:52:25,280 --> 00:52:27,820
Here, we were not
training a new verifier.

898
00:52:27,820 --> 00:52:31,790
We were thinking, can we reduce
the generation-verification gap

899
00:52:31,790 --> 00:52:35,130
by using inference compute
and inference scaling,

900
00:52:35,130 --> 00:52:39,730
and specifically by using
an ensemble of verifiers?

901
00:52:39,730 --> 00:52:43,990
Because when we say "weak," we
just assume that no verifier is

902
00:52:43,990 --> 00:52:44,552
perfect.

903
00:52:44,552 --> 00:52:45,510
So that's the weakness.

904
00:52:45,510 --> 00:52:48,450
We didn't purposefully
choose bad verifiers.

905
00:52:48,450 --> 00:52:50,570
These are the best verifiers
that are out there.

906
00:52:50,570 --> 00:52:52,430
But we are using
an ensemble of them

907
00:52:52,430 --> 00:52:57,730
to create a single, much
more capable verifier.

908
00:53:02,630 --> 00:53:05,070
So the property
of these verifiers

909
00:53:05,070 --> 00:53:10,370
is that they do correlate with
the true label of the solution,

910
00:53:10,370 --> 00:53:12,590
but they have imperfections.

911
00:53:12,590 --> 00:53:14,770
And there are two types
of these verifiers.

912
00:53:14,770 --> 00:53:18,910
We learned about PRMs and ORMs,
but another one that we briefly

913
00:53:18,910 --> 00:53:22,790
discussed a few minutes
ago is that we can

914
00:53:22,790 --> 00:53:25,830
use LLMs themselves as a judge.

915
00:53:25,830 --> 00:53:29,350
We can show it an
answer and ask it,

916
00:53:29,350 --> 00:53:31,160
do you think this
is correct or not?

917
00:53:31,160 --> 00:53:33,120
The prompt could
be literally that.

918
00:53:33,120 --> 00:53:36,300
Or we can have the model
use tools and use rubrics

919
00:53:36,300 --> 00:53:39,800
and all that to generate
a score for a model.

920
00:53:39,800 --> 00:53:42,380
So that's a whole
category of verifiers

921
00:53:42,380 --> 00:53:46,700
that we can use on top
of just PRMs and ORMs.

922
00:53:46,700 --> 00:53:52,220
So here, this first graph just
shows how ensembling and using

923
00:53:52,220 --> 00:53:55,380
a number of verifiers
that are-- they

924
00:53:55,380 --> 00:53:59,955
could be trained by different
labs on different data sets.

925
00:53:59,955 --> 00:54:01,580
And there are all
these language models

926
00:54:01,580 --> 00:54:03,660
that take a query
as input and give it

927
00:54:03,660 --> 00:54:05,620
a score on the output side.

928
00:54:05,620 --> 00:54:09,860
Here, we are seeing
these four data sets.

929
00:54:09,860 --> 00:54:14,220
So in this case, we are showing
the performance of top 1,

930
00:54:14,220 --> 00:54:17,540
versus top 5, versus
top 10 verifiers,

931
00:54:17,540 --> 00:54:21,460
based on how good they are
in some ranking system.

932
00:54:21,460 --> 00:54:31,250
Overall, it seems like just
ensembling them with each other

933
00:54:31,250 --> 00:54:36,650
and using them at test
time does improve things,

934
00:54:36,650 --> 00:54:41,810
but not necessarily
in a monotonic way.

935
00:54:41,810 --> 00:54:44,250
But the thing that
always helped was that,

936
00:54:44,250 --> 00:54:49,250
if we train a model,
a way for them

937
00:54:49,250 --> 00:54:52,250
to add them together--
so assign weights

938
00:54:52,250 --> 00:54:54,710
for different members
of the ensemble,

939
00:54:54,710 --> 00:54:56,250
if we have labels from them.

940
00:54:56,250 --> 00:55:00,510
Here, we have two methods, Naive
Bayes or logistic regression.

941
00:55:00,510 --> 00:55:02,430
These are very simple methods.

942
00:55:02,430 --> 00:55:05,850
Basically, you add a
single weight per verifier

943
00:55:05,850 --> 00:55:07,730
to score them together.

944
00:55:07,730 --> 00:55:13,190
And if you have a
training set with labels,

945
00:55:13,190 --> 00:55:15,910
you can basically come
up with these weights,

946
00:55:15,910 --> 00:55:19,250
and now these results are on
the test set, corresponding

947
00:55:19,250 --> 00:55:20,830
to those tasks.

948
00:55:20,830 --> 00:55:25,990
So you basically say, for
MATH500, I have a set of labels.

949
00:55:25,990 --> 00:55:29,540
I use that to average my
verifiers, find the weights,

950
00:55:29,540 --> 00:55:33,320
and then I use these
averages on test data.

951
00:55:33,320 --> 00:55:38,100
So it seems like it does
give a lot of improvement,

952
00:55:38,100 --> 00:55:41,040
just this simple task.

953
00:55:41,040 --> 00:55:43,500
Now, you can do other things.

954
00:55:43,500 --> 00:55:47,600
So the way Weaver works is by
this "score, weight, and select"

955
00:55:47,600 --> 00:55:48,260
approach.

956
00:55:48,260 --> 00:55:50,640
So, in the first
step, we are going

957
00:55:50,640 --> 00:55:54,480
to score outputs
of the verifiers

958
00:55:54,480 --> 00:55:58,360
and normalize them so they're
all on the same scale,

959
00:55:58,360 --> 00:56:03,180
and filter out the
low-quality verifiers.

960
00:56:03,180 --> 00:56:05,200
So in this setup,
even, we are assuming

961
00:56:05,200 --> 00:56:07,680
we have access to
very limited labels

962
00:56:07,680 --> 00:56:09,840
that we can say, OK,
these verifiers are just

963
00:56:09,840 --> 00:56:11,400
so bad against our labels.

964
00:56:11,400 --> 00:56:14,080
We just remove them
from our ensemble.

965
00:56:14,080 --> 00:56:17,240
We have noticed that this
is a very important step.

966
00:56:17,240 --> 00:56:20,080
Your verifiers should be
above a certain quality

967
00:56:20,080 --> 00:56:24,340
to even be let in the
pool of verifiers.

968
00:56:24,340 --> 00:56:26,610
So then you have your verifiers.

969
00:56:26,610 --> 00:56:27,850
You have your LLM judges.

970
00:56:27,850 --> 00:56:29,090
You have your reward models.

971
00:56:29,090 --> 00:56:31,190
And then you get these
scores from them,

972
00:56:31,190 --> 00:56:35,110
whether it's 0 or 1 or the
log prob of correctness

973
00:56:35,110 --> 00:56:38,190
per verifier.

974
00:56:38,190 --> 00:56:45,390
And then what Weaver did was to
use weak supervision to estimate

975
00:56:45,390 --> 00:56:51,030
the verifier accuracy with very
small data, labeled "data."

976
00:56:51,030 --> 00:56:55,230
And then we use those weights
to combine the verifiers' output

977
00:56:55,230 --> 00:56:58,070
and assign a score.

978
00:56:58,070 --> 00:57:01,990
I go briefly through how this
weak to strong supervision

979
00:57:01,990 --> 00:57:02,490
works.

980
00:57:02,490 --> 00:57:05,670
Some of you, if you have taken
deep-learning courses, or even

981
00:57:05,670 --> 00:57:11,210
CS229S last time,
we talked about it,

982
00:57:11,210 --> 00:57:13,010
about weak to
strong supervision.

983
00:57:13,010 --> 00:57:15,470
There's a whole
body of work here.

984
00:57:15,470 --> 00:57:21,510
Weaver was
specifically motivated

985
00:57:21,510 --> 00:57:26,660
by the work by Snorkel, by Alex
Ratner et al., and other work

986
00:57:26,660 --> 00:57:28,380
from Stanford.

987
00:57:28,380 --> 00:57:32,660
So here is how we have these
weak verifiers, like weak signal

988
00:57:32,660 --> 00:57:34,860
from our verifiers, and
combine them together

989
00:57:34,860 --> 00:57:36,780
to get a stronger signal.

990
00:57:36,780 --> 00:57:38,320
The setup is the following.

991
00:57:38,320 --> 00:57:40,060
We have n queries.

992
00:57:40,060 --> 00:57:43,640
For each query, we
create k solutions.

993
00:57:43,640 --> 00:57:46,260
And then let's assume
we have m verifiers.

994
00:57:46,260 --> 00:57:51,340
So we have a total of n
times k times m labels.

995
00:57:51,340 --> 00:58:00,260
And our goal here is to get
this probability of query i,

996
00:58:00,260 --> 00:58:03,980
sample j equal to 1,
given all the labels

997
00:58:03,980 --> 00:58:05,760
that we get from
all the verifiers.

998
00:58:05,760 --> 00:58:10,140
So how do we estimate this
probability correctly?

999
00:58:10,140 --> 00:58:12,980
The assumption in Weaver
is that each verifier

1000
00:58:12,980 --> 00:58:17,780
captures independent
aspect of the correctness.

1001
00:58:17,780 --> 00:58:20,420
So this is a key
assumption that we made.

1002
00:58:20,420 --> 00:58:23,490
To some extent,
the reason we get--

1003
00:58:23,490 --> 00:58:24,750
so think about it this way.

1004
00:58:24,750 --> 00:58:27,530
If you have a pool of
verifiers, if all of them

1005
00:58:27,530 --> 00:58:30,870
agree on the score that
they give to any sample,

1006
00:58:30,870 --> 00:58:33,570
then we are not learning
anything new from them,

1007
00:58:33,570 --> 00:58:36,970
if all of them are
100% or the same score.

1008
00:58:36,970 --> 00:58:41,770
The signal here is on
similarity and dissimilarity

1009
00:58:41,770 --> 00:58:45,450
across this whole set of
verifiers with each other.

1010
00:58:45,450 --> 00:58:49,810
So, under this assumption,
we can write this probability

1011
00:58:49,810 --> 00:58:54,370
for the response being correct,
given the labels that we have,

1012
00:58:54,370 --> 00:58:56,870
and then we use two
sets of equations.

1013
00:58:56,870 --> 00:59:00,850
Again, this the
first equation is--

1014
00:59:00,850 --> 00:59:02,650
we can write it
this way because we

1015
00:59:02,650 --> 00:59:05,010
assume every two
verifier i and j are

1016
00:59:05,010 --> 00:59:06,550
independent from each other.

1017
00:59:06,550 --> 00:59:11,650
The second equation
is just the Bayes--

1018
00:59:11,650 --> 00:59:13,430
the way probability works.

1019
00:59:13,430 --> 00:59:15,270
There's no assumption
in writing this.

1020
00:59:15,270 --> 00:59:22,040
And given these two, we
can write an optimization

1021
00:59:22,040 --> 00:59:25,120
and come up with the
weights that we can assign

1022
00:59:25,120 --> 00:59:29,880
to each of the verifiers.

1023
00:59:29,880 --> 00:59:34,600
And here, we are comparing the
results with a naive ensemble

1024
00:59:34,600 --> 00:59:37,640
approach, and it turns
out that it does help.

1025
00:59:37,640 --> 00:59:41,760
In some cases, the gains are
more significant-- in GPQA

1026
00:59:41,760 --> 00:59:45,880
Diamond and MATH and MLU Pro.

1027
00:59:45,880 --> 00:59:50,300
And each of these problems that
are relatively hard problems--

1028
00:59:50,300 --> 00:59:55,660
for example, the GPQA, our
baseline, is already low,

1029
00:59:55,660 --> 00:59:59,240
but the verifier-- this way
of weak to strong aggregation

1030
00:59:59,240 --> 01:00:03,520
of responses is helping
getting a large boost,

1031
01:00:03,520 --> 01:00:07,160
over naive ensembling of them.

1032
01:00:07,160 --> 01:00:09,160
Now, there are different
ways that we can

1033
01:00:09,160 --> 01:00:11,500
scale the verification compute.

1034
01:00:11,500 --> 01:00:13,620
We can sample more generations.

1035
01:00:13,620 --> 01:00:17,400
So, instead of 10 samples,
we can do 100 or 1,000.

1036
01:00:17,400 --> 01:00:21,570
We can use larger models for
generation and verification.

1037
01:00:21,570 --> 01:00:27,510
We can increase the number
of verifiers in the pool.

1038
01:00:27,510 --> 01:00:30,190
And in general, there
are different ways

1039
01:00:30,190 --> 01:00:33,630
to assign flops
or scale inference

1040
01:00:33,630 --> 01:00:37,670
compute for solving a problem.

1041
01:00:37,670 --> 01:00:41,790
Here, we are comparing some of
these methods with each other.

1042
01:00:41,790 --> 01:00:49,270
So the dashed, dark red
line is the Pass@K Oracle,

1043
01:00:49,270 --> 01:00:54,690
meaning that if we had
an Oracle selector,

1044
01:00:54,690 --> 01:00:57,048
that would give us
the correct answer.

1045
01:00:57,048 --> 01:00:58,590
We don't have that,
and that's why we

1046
01:00:58,590 --> 01:01:01,950
are training these verifiers.

1047
01:01:01,950 --> 01:01:04,690
The purple one is
Weaver Supervised,

1048
01:01:04,690 --> 01:01:07,770
and that's assumed that we have
a large body of labeled data

1049
01:01:07,770 --> 01:01:10,230
and we can use them to
learn these weights.

1050
01:01:10,230 --> 01:01:12,530
The blue one is
the Unsupervised.

1051
01:01:12,530 --> 01:01:15,510
In this case, we're
using 1% of the training

1052
01:01:15,510 --> 01:01:18,700
labels for each data set.

1053
01:01:18,700 --> 01:01:20,960
The orange one is
the Naive Ensemble,

1054
01:01:20,960 --> 01:01:26,500
meaning we just average
them, average the verifiers

1055
01:01:26,500 --> 01:01:28,720
without any special treatment.

1056
01:01:28,720 --> 01:01:31,860
We still are
filtering and keeping

1057
01:01:31,860 --> 01:01:33,900
the good verifiers in the loop.

1058
01:01:33,900 --> 01:01:36,500
And then all of these
are significantly better

1059
01:01:36,500 --> 01:01:40,660
than methods such as Majority
Voting or Multi-Agent

1060
01:01:40,660 --> 01:01:41,880
Verification.

1061
01:01:41,880 --> 01:01:47,140
And Multi-Agent
Verification, or MAV--

1062
01:01:47,140 --> 01:01:50,380
it's just based on
prompting LLMs and asking

1063
01:01:50,380 --> 01:01:52,820
them to score a
given response, based

1064
01:01:52,820 --> 01:01:56,120
on different kind of
aspects that is produced,

1065
01:01:56,120 --> 01:01:59,940
so a rubric-based, which
doesn't do that much

1066
01:01:59,940 --> 01:02:02,540
better-- or actually
worse, in these two cases--

1067
01:02:02,540 --> 01:02:03,960
than Majority Voting.

1068
01:02:09,420 --> 01:02:13,340
But something to notice
here is the drastic gains

1069
01:02:13,340 --> 01:02:17,050
that the model can have,
going from something

1070
01:02:17,050 --> 01:02:25,090
like slightly over 40% to over
70% on these hard problems

1071
01:02:25,090 --> 01:02:29,850
and, in this case, matching
a model like o3-mini.

1072
01:02:29,850 --> 01:02:32,650
So the thing that
is interesting here

1073
01:02:32,650 --> 01:02:39,010
is that we can use this weak
to strong supervision to reduce

1074
01:02:39,010 --> 01:02:41,550
the gap between model classes.

1075
01:02:41,550 --> 01:02:43,450
So what we are
seeing here is that,

1076
01:02:43,450 --> 01:02:48,130
if we take a generator model
of Llama 3.1 8B Instruct,

1077
01:02:48,130 --> 01:02:53,570
and the verifier model which is
the pool of verifiers that are

1078
01:02:53,570 --> 01:02:59,890
8B and below, we get an average
of 70% on these data set.

1079
01:02:59,890 --> 01:03:05,090
And this is almost comparable
to the accuracy that we get with

1080
01:03:05,090 --> 01:03:10,330
majority voting, but when
our models are at 70B.

1081
01:03:10,330 --> 01:03:19,800
So we are making the model,
making this 8B model,

1082
01:03:19,800 --> 01:03:24,680
roughly the same as the 70B
class by just doing this

1083
01:03:24,680 --> 01:03:27,300
inference scaling and
using the verifier.

1084
01:03:27,300 --> 01:03:29,840
And in this case,
unlike what we saw

1085
01:03:29,840 --> 01:03:32,080
in most of the
previous lecture, we

1086
01:03:32,080 --> 01:03:33,700
are talking about
the end results.

1087
01:03:33,700 --> 01:03:35,540
We're not talking
about the coverage.

1088
01:03:35,540 --> 01:03:41,600
We're talking about the solution
accuracy of the entire system.

1089
01:03:41,600 --> 01:03:45,260
And here again, in this case,
for when we do the same,

1090
01:03:45,260 --> 01:03:49,600
apply the same generation
and verification to 70B class

1091
01:03:49,600 --> 01:03:53,760
of models, we are getting
an average accuracy of 86.2%

1092
01:03:53,760 --> 01:03:55,020
on these data sets.

1093
01:03:55,020 --> 01:04:04,320
And this is very comparable
to o3-mini, which has a much--

1094
01:04:04,320 --> 01:04:07,680
which is like a proprietary
model and in general,

1095
01:04:07,680 --> 01:04:10,540
is a different class of models.

1096
01:04:10,540 --> 01:04:13,470
So this shows how much
verification, or this work

1097
01:04:13,470 --> 01:04:16,550
and verification, can
help with the results.

1098
01:04:16,550 --> 01:04:20,070
Another work that was
done on increasing

1099
01:04:20,070 --> 01:04:22,370
the usefulness of
something like Weaver

1100
01:04:22,370 --> 01:04:25,230
is that a challenge with
ensembling verifiers

1101
01:04:25,230 --> 01:04:27,670
is that you have a ton
of models that now you

1102
01:04:27,670 --> 01:04:31,110
need to run for each
solution to get a score,

1103
01:04:31,110 --> 01:04:34,430
and then to average them.

1104
01:04:34,430 --> 01:04:36,950
And that increases
the cost, especially

1105
01:04:36,950 --> 01:04:40,970
if, instead of one sample per
query, you have 100 samples,

1106
01:04:40,970 --> 01:04:43,810
and then you want to
create all these scores.

1107
01:04:43,810 --> 01:04:49,110
So instead, the proposal here
is that, what if we do train

1108
01:04:49,110 --> 01:04:53,950
Weaver once and then distill
it into a much smaller model?

1109
01:04:53,950 --> 01:04:58,430
And that's how, instead of
running each of these LLM judges

1110
01:04:58,430 --> 01:05:03,957
or reward models per sample, we
just take this distilled LLM,

1111
01:05:03,957 --> 01:05:06,290
which-- in this case, we
showed that it could be really,

1112
01:05:06,290 --> 01:05:10,300
really tiny, like something
like 400 million parameters,

1113
01:05:10,300 --> 01:05:12,140
as opposed to the
original model,

1114
01:05:12,140 --> 01:05:15,860
which was in the 70B range.

1115
01:05:15,860 --> 01:05:18,040
And it turns out that,
with this distilled model,

1116
01:05:18,040 --> 01:05:26,120
we can capture 97% of accuracy
of this large pool of verifiers,

1117
01:05:26,120 --> 01:05:31,100
but significantly-- in
this case, 99%-plus--

1118
01:05:31,100 --> 01:05:33,700
use less compute at test time.

1119
01:05:33,700 --> 01:05:38,340
And these distilled version, and
the original version-- they're

1120
01:05:38,340 --> 01:05:42,180
all open-sourced, and the
checkpoints are available,

1121
01:05:42,180 --> 01:05:44,260
if any of you are
interested in working

1122
01:05:44,260 --> 01:05:49,880
with them in your agentic or
test-time scaling projects.

1123
01:05:54,060 --> 01:05:55,880
And here is another graph.

1124
01:05:55,880 --> 01:05:58,740
Here is the distilled one,
which is the light blue one,

1125
01:05:58,740 --> 01:06:02,280
and the original Weaver
is the dark blue one.

1126
01:06:02,280 --> 01:06:06,420
And we are comparing, again,
the efficiency, the success

1127
01:06:06,420 --> 01:06:10,650
rate over the inference compute,
the total inference compute

1128
01:06:10,650 --> 01:06:14,170
that we are spending
on this data set.

1129
01:06:14,170 --> 01:06:18,170
And the distilled
one, obviously,

1130
01:06:18,170 --> 01:06:20,690
is significantly more efficient.

1131
01:06:20,690 --> 01:06:23,450
But even the original
Weaver-- because it reaches

1132
01:06:23,450 --> 01:06:27,150
higher levels of
accuracy at those levels,

1133
01:06:27,150 --> 01:06:30,890
it becomes more flop-efficient
than models like naive ensemble

1134
01:06:30,890 --> 01:06:32,230
and majority voting.

1135
01:06:35,730 --> 01:06:39,930
And let's do a quick recap.

1136
01:06:39,930 --> 01:06:45,610
So today we covered
four papers that

1137
01:06:45,610 --> 01:06:49,610
also show the trajectory
of how this work, research

1138
01:06:49,610 --> 01:06:53,510
on verification has progressed
over the last four years.

1139
01:06:53,510 --> 01:07:00,010
We talked about how verification
can improve training,

1140
01:07:00,010 --> 01:07:04,610
can improve the outcome
and the quality of results,

1141
01:07:04,610 --> 01:07:07,720
both during training
and inference.

1142
01:07:07,720 --> 01:07:13,360
Process reward seems to be
very useful and, overall, very

1143
01:07:13,360 --> 01:07:17,340
effective, and more so
than the outcome rewards,

1144
01:07:17,340 --> 01:07:20,200
but perhaps we need to
combine them to get the best

1145
01:07:20,200 --> 01:07:23,600
results, both PRMs and ORMs.

1146
01:07:23,600 --> 01:07:27,620
And we also, in [INAUDIBLE],
learned that scaling--

1147
01:07:27,620 --> 01:07:28,860
you need a lot of data.

1148
01:07:28,860 --> 01:07:31,780
And the more data you throw
in the verifier training,

1149
01:07:31,780 --> 01:07:33,800
it becomes better,
and you can use it

1150
01:07:33,800 --> 01:07:38,440
during your RL fine-tuning
to increase the quality

1151
01:07:38,440 --> 01:07:40,400
of your generator as well.

1152
01:07:40,400 --> 01:07:44,920
And the Weaver work took
a whole different approach

1153
01:07:44,920 --> 01:07:48,080
to verification,
and that was just

1154
01:07:48,080 --> 01:07:50,480
doing this weak
supervised optimization

1155
01:07:50,480 --> 01:07:52,860
on an ensemble of verifiers.

1156
01:07:52,860 --> 01:07:55,260
And in a way, we are
using test-time scaling,

1157
01:07:55,260 --> 01:07:57,440
but by bringing more
verifiers, rather

1158
01:07:57,440 --> 01:08:02,300
than sampling a single verifier
more to get the results.

1159
01:08:02,300 --> 01:08:05,160
And it turns out we can
distill that and capture

1160
01:08:05,160 --> 01:08:09,630
a whole lot of the quality
from a much smaller model.

1161
01:08:09,630 --> 01:08:13,030
And with that, I can
conclude the class.

1162
01:08:13,030 --> 01:08:15,390
Any questions?

1163
01:08:15,390 --> 01:08:15,950
Yes?

1164
01:08:15,950 --> 01:08:21,270
[? Are ?] all the papers
were on [INAUDIBLE] reasoning

1165
01:08:21,270 --> 01:08:21,930
benchmarks.

1166
01:08:21,930 --> 01:08:23,555
Do you think there's
anything else that

1167
01:08:23,555 --> 01:08:25,790
could work for our
project, other ways

1168
01:08:25,790 --> 01:08:28,910
to evaluate the models?

1169
01:08:28,910 --> 01:08:29,689
Like coding?

1170
01:08:29,689 --> 01:08:31,870
Other ways to
evaluate the model--

1171
01:08:31,870 --> 01:08:34,350
you mean other
areas, like coding,

1172
01:08:34,350 --> 01:08:38,130
would be a very interesting
area for reasoning.

1173
01:08:38,130 --> 01:08:41,710
So there were some
coding problems

1174
01:08:41,710 --> 01:08:45,870
in some of the benchmarks
here, so it's not just math.

1175
01:08:45,870 --> 01:08:51,170
But coding is also a
very interesting area

1176
01:08:51,170 --> 01:08:52,229
for verification.

1177
01:08:52,229 --> 01:08:55,130
We will talk about code monkeys.

1178
01:08:55,130 --> 01:08:57,590
This is a paper that--

1179
01:08:57,590 --> 01:09:00,010
instead of directly
training a verifier,

1180
01:09:00,010 --> 01:09:03,510
you can make the model
create a test-time system

1181
01:09:03,510 --> 01:09:06,420
to make the model
generate unit tests,

1182
01:09:06,420 --> 01:09:08,520
and those unit tests
become your verifier.

1183
01:09:08,520 --> 01:09:11,060
So that's an entirely
different approach.

1184
01:09:11,060 --> 01:09:14,100
But these kind of
verifications should also

1185
01:09:14,100 --> 01:09:16,960
be useful for any kind
of reasoning task.

1186
01:09:22,899 --> 01:09:24,500
Yes?

1187
01:09:24,500 --> 01:09:27,260
I'm curious, how do
generation-verification

1188
01:09:27,260 --> 01:09:34,800
[INAUDIBLE] using models that
internalize this [INAUDIBLE]?

1189
01:09:37,140 --> 01:09:40,120
OK, so the question is,
for reasoning model,

1190
01:09:40,120 --> 01:09:42,540
how does the
generation-verification,

1191
01:09:42,540 --> 01:09:45,319
or this kind of test-time
scalings work for them?

1192
01:09:45,319 --> 01:09:48,260
It actually helps
them as well, still.

1193
01:09:48,260 --> 01:09:50,700
But the reasoning model--

1194
01:09:50,700 --> 01:09:53,120
we're going to talk
about them in this class.

1195
01:09:53,120 --> 01:09:55,700
But basically, what they're
doing-- a big part of it

1196
01:09:55,700 --> 01:10:00,540
is to generate these reasoning
steps, use the reward for it,

1197
01:10:00,540 --> 01:10:04,870
and do an RL loop, and
generate positive trajectories,

1198
01:10:04,870 --> 01:10:08,130
and then use that positive
trajectories as part of training

1199
01:10:08,130 --> 01:10:09,150
for the next round.

1200
01:10:09,150 --> 01:10:13,370
So the test-time scaling
is inherently used

1201
01:10:13,370 --> 01:10:17,290
to generate data and generate
the training process.

1202
01:10:17,290 --> 01:10:21,090
But they still would
benefit because they still

1203
01:10:21,090 --> 01:10:25,170
could explore different
parts of the solution space

1204
01:10:25,170 --> 01:10:27,970
with more sampling.

1205
01:10:27,970 --> 01:10:34,390
[INAUDIBLE] existing
knowledge, using [INAUDIBLE].

1206
01:10:34,390 --> 01:10:37,110
Do you think, in the
future, these two paradigms

1207
01:10:37,110 --> 01:10:40,610
will converge, or
do you think maybe

1208
01:10:40,610 --> 01:10:44,770
repeated sampling will
only be used for generating

1209
01:10:44,770 --> 01:10:48,210
training data for [INAUDIBLE]?

1210
01:10:48,210 --> 01:10:51,450
So the question is, would
we converge to a future

1211
01:10:51,450 --> 01:10:53,690
where these kind of
test-time scaling, repeated

1212
01:10:53,690 --> 01:10:55,970
sampling [INAUDIBLE]
just for training time

1213
01:10:55,970 --> 01:10:59,290
to create the data,
and then, at test time,

1214
01:10:59,290 --> 01:11:01,580
we just ask the model once?

1215
01:11:01,580 --> 01:11:03,400
I think that's the
direction we want to go.

1216
01:11:03,400 --> 01:11:07,120
We want the model to be really,
really good at pass at one.

1217
01:11:07,120 --> 01:11:09,440
The first time we ask
it to do something,

1218
01:11:09,440 --> 01:11:11,480
we want it to generate,
and that increases

1219
01:11:11,480 --> 01:11:13,020
the efficiency and all that.

1220
01:11:13,020 --> 01:11:14,480
That would be the hope.

1221
01:11:14,480 --> 01:11:18,460
One challenge, though,
with making the model--

1222
01:11:18,460 --> 01:11:21,000
to make the log
probs of the model--

1223
01:11:21,000 --> 01:11:25,120
to sharpen through one
answer is that the creativity

1224
01:11:25,120 --> 01:11:27,880
and diversity of solutions
may be lost somehow

1225
01:11:27,880 --> 01:11:30,360
during the training.

1226
01:11:30,360 --> 01:11:37,480
So surprisingly, we
still like models

1227
01:11:37,480 --> 01:11:39,720
have a lot of
creativity and diversity

1228
01:11:39,720 --> 01:11:41,620
in the way that they think.

1229
01:11:41,620 --> 01:11:46,240
And that's a good feature that
we want to have in the models.

1230
01:11:46,240 --> 01:11:48,240
Yes?

1231
01:11:48,240 --> 01:11:50,600
Does it matter if
generator and verifiers

1232
01:11:50,600 --> 01:11:52,700
have very different
architectures-- for example,

1233
01:11:52,700 --> 01:11:55,280
if the verifier is a mini
version of the generator,

1234
01:11:55,280 --> 01:11:59,350
versus if they're very
different architectures?

1235
01:11:59,350 --> 01:12:00,130
Does it matter--

1236
01:12:00,130 --> 01:12:04,430
Which one is empirically better?

1237
01:12:04,430 --> 01:12:07,870
OK, so the question is if
the generator and verifier

1238
01:12:07,870 --> 01:12:10,350
is from the same family or not.

1239
01:12:10,350 --> 01:12:13,430
That's a good research question.

1240
01:12:13,430 --> 01:12:16,670
I think, in general,
it's interesting.

1241
01:12:16,670 --> 01:12:19,590
Models like their
own generations

1242
01:12:19,590 --> 01:12:21,950
and their own way
of interpreting

1243
01:12:21,950 --> 01:12:26,950
results much better than using
a different family of models.

1244
01:12:26,950 --> 01:12:30,150
We are going to talk
about a paper that

1245
01:12:30,150 --> 01:12:34,090
talks about how certain
models can benefit from,

1246
01:12:34,090 --> 01:12:37,190
like a different class of
verifiers over themselves.

1247
01:12:37,190 --> 01:12:41,270
But specifically, I am
unaware of a study that

1248
01:12:41,270 --> 01:12:45,870
has looked into that specific
question of same size

1249
01:12:45,870 --> 01:12:50,430
and family, versus different
size, different family-- how

1250
01:12:50,430 --> 01:12:53,540
that changes the
behavior of the model.
